item(45,[45,48],'unpursued implication',[126,128,142],['1092-1102','1201-1214','1968-1987'],'Supposons que ∃γ∈k, γχ=2','For a fixed-endpoint comparison of complementary split-coordinate constructions, the translation v=−γb works if γχ=2. This means2∈χk, not χ invertible. For χ=2,γ=1 works over every base ring.','On the coherent plus branch b′=b+χU the square defect is(2−γχ)U. This restricts the comparison, not the existence of the product itself.','agrees','The condition is explicitly introduced as a hypothesis at128 and repeated at142. Entry48 repeats the same necessary distinction.')
item(46,[46],'reading question',[128],['1191-1192'],'T_{−Ũ}=−T_{−U}=T_U^{-1}','For T_U(η,ζ)=(η,ζ+Uη), its inverse is T_−U; −T_−U differs by endpoint signs. The frozen chain is not an equality under fixed endpoint identifications except in suitable2-torsion circumstances.','The live minus might belong to a different identification or be an uncancelled draft sign; the ink must decide. Matrix discrepancy is(−2,0;2U,−2).','agrees','The note correctly reports the surviving minus and does not silently delete it.')
item(47,[47],'reading question',[128],['1171-1189','1194-1210'],'tilde b=tilde b1 tilde b2=b1b2; tilde b=−b','Swapping both input coefficients gives(+b1b2), whereas the complementary splitting of the output is assigned−b. These two uses of a tilded b require distinct identifications.','Their coincidence is not assumed. This may be a convention switch rather than a false product formula.','agrees','Both literal signs are present; no source normalization is authorized without checking the ink and conventions.')
item(49,[49],'idea of the earlier run',[128],['1212-1214'],'v=−γb','The auxiliary choice γ may affect the comparison: over Z/4 withχ=2,b=1,γ=1 or3 gives translations3 or1. Both change b to−b but differ by the nonzeroχ-torsion automorphism2.','This is a new explicit example in the earlier notes, rechecked here. The distinguished choiceγ=1 atχ=2 remains available.','agrees','The computation demonstrates choice-dependence of the displayed recipe; it is not a refutation of every possible canonical comparison.')
item(50,[50],'reading question',[130],['1274-1312'],'π_(p1,p2)(η,ζ)+U(η)','The same-coordinate definitions b_i′=b_i+χU_i and U=b1U2+U1b2+χU1U2 imply b1′b2′−b1b2=χU. The final line writes only+U.','Possible missingχ or implicit coordinate transport; the same-coordinate discrepancy is(χ−1)U. No literal correction is adopted.','agrees','The mismatch follows from the earlier definitions on the same page, independently of the later packet.')
item(51,[51,52],'reading question',[132,134],['1382-1449','1504-1575'],'Je dis que U″=U+U′','The product transition law satisfies U″=U+U′ with b_i′=b_i+χU_i and U_i″=U_i+U_i′. The sheet also uses an incompatible minus convention; substituting it into the same plus product law leaves2χ(U1U2′+U2U1′).','The exact residual is the earlier run’s sign test, freshly recomputed. A consistent cocycle supports descent of local data but is not all monoidal coherence.','agrees','The source itself mixes plus and minus conventions and later renames increments. Entry52 is the computation of this same issue.')
item(53,[53],'reading question',[132],['1370-1371','1421','1428-1429'],'p″3|p″2; U″2=(U2|U′2)+U′2','The two-factor successive-change calculation suggests index2 at the upper vertex, U2 as the first summand in U″2, and a prime on the base b2 in the second stage.','The last proposed prime is conditional on U2′ meaning the second increment, not the total increment.','agrees','Adjacent first-factor equations supply a mathematical reading test, but cannot decide the ink.')
item(55,[55],'reading question',[134,136],['1537-1554','1653-1665'],'Hom^!(E,L); [π′1,f′1]′','The special dual consists, tentatively, of maps E→L with scalar restriction to L. If L is not faithful, the scalar is not determined by the map; one needs pairs(f,λ) or additional faithfulness. The three primes in the bracket formula lack local definitions.','The later shifted-operator notation must not be substituted for these symbols without proof. For an invertible L, scalar uniqueness follows.','agrees','The set-builder is unfinished and Hom! really has an exclamation mark. The notes properly identify missing definitions.')
item(56,[56],'self-flagged step',[134,136],['1529-1535','1647-1676'],'signe étrange!','The products of quotient-linear maps Ui have−χU1⊗U2, whereas submodule vectors ui have+χu1⊗u2. These are distinct input types.','This follows from the bilinear functional-product formula when λ1=λ2=0. The dual-extension endpoint sign explains one origin; it does not reconcile every draft convention.','agrees','The author’s warning is explicit; changing the negative sign to the vector formula would erase a type distinction.')
item(57,[57,59],'self-flagged step',[136,138,140],['1638-1643','1727-1751','1833-1859'],'Vérifions; OK; p(x1*x2)=x1*x2−(x1−p1x1)*(x2−p2x2)','For fi:E_i→L_i restricting toλ_i id, the tensor prescription is λ1π1⊗f2+f1⊗λ2π2−χf1⊗f2 and the scalar-boundary prescription isλ1λ2 id. It agrees on the mixed boundary. Setting fi=pi gives the product splitting.','We verify both mixed strata and overlap; the source explicitly checks only one. The later rewrite with an overallχ is crossed out, not a live normalization.','agrees','The cancellation at138 is correct. Entry59 is the same functional formula specialized to the surviving product splitting and records the withdrawn rewrite.')
item(58,[58],'reading question',[138],['1754-1758'],'(1/χ)π1*π2=π','The integral identity is π1*π2=χπ on both generator strata. Writing1/χ is justified as division only whenχ is invertible; otherwise it can at most denote divisibility by an independently defined map.','Uniqueness of the quotient fails whenχ annihilates maps. The entire construction is not restricted to invertibleχ.','agrees','The written fraction is retained and the exact integral meaning is separated from stronger cancellation.')
item(60,[60],'reading question',[140],['1863-1886'],'2−χ=1','The displayed restriction of the functional product gives(2χ−χ)λ1λ2u1⊗u2=χλ1λ2u1⊗u2. It does not requireχ=1; the isolated literal2−χ=1 is incompatible with the general calculation.','Possible lost factor or abandoned normalization. This is a mathematical alarm for scan inspection, not a global assumption.','agrees','The surrounding live formula and crossed-out alternative are both preserved; the note correctly declines to imposeχ=1.')
item(61,[61],'unproved claim',[142],['1939-1966'],'Si on a pu définir F11','A middle block satisfying the two prescribed composites yields a strict morphism of short exact sequences with identities on both ends, hence an isomorphism. Constructing that middle block remains a separate problem.','The last implication is the short five lemma; existence is not a consequence of specifying boundary values.','agrees','The conditional opening is explicit and cannot be omitted.')
item(62,[62],'unproved claim',[142],['1968-1987'],'χ id−(π′1+π′2)+γπ′1⊗π′2; γχ=2','A candidate comparison block is χI−(P1+P2)+γP1P2 with γχ=2, subject to identifying the doubtful primed operators. Atχ=2 it becomes I+σ1⊗σ2 forσ_i=I−P_i.','Identifying P_i with the localχ-retraction endomorphisms is a declared reconstruction. Tensor checks do not alone certify all four comparison squares or naturality.','agrees','The scalar restriction andχ=2 specialization are printed; no complete global descent is provided.')
item(63,[63],'idea of the earlier run',[142],['1974-1987'],'équivaut ... −id_L1 et +id_M1','For 0→L --a→E→M→0, a mapπ:E→L withπa=2id corresponds to an endomorphismσ=I−aπ inducing−id on L and+id on M; suchσ is automatically involutive. Conversely I−σ factors uniquely througha and recoversπ.','This exact-sequence proof, developed by the earlier run and rechecked here, uses no inverse of2; it is not an eigenspace decomposition.','agrees','The page gives the compressed equivalence. The note supplies precisely its integral interpretation.')
item(64,[64,65],'unproved claim',[144],['2021-2052'],'f(x1*x2)=f1(x1)*f2(x2)','The apparent proposal for arbitrary submodule-preserving endomorphisms is too broad: one also needsπ_i f_i=f_i^! π_i for descent through the product relation. Over any nonzero field,χ=1 and the shear m1↦m1+ℓ1 fixesL1 but sends the relation m1*ℓ2=0 toℓ1⊗ℓ2≠0.','The counterexample is conditional on reading the uncertain initial letter asf_i and on the displayed data being the complete hypothesis. Compatible morphisms remain valid.','agrees','The note accurately distinguishes the rough proposal, its missing compatibility, and the explicit earlier-run counterexample. Entry65 is merged as its refutation certificate.')
item(66,[66],'unpursued implication',[144],['2061-2105'],'λ1π1(x1)=f1(x1)χ','For fi:E_i→L_i with scalar restrictionsλ_i, the final equation is additional compatibility with the targetχ-retractionχid. Scalar restriction onL_i alone does not imply it onE_i.','Neitherχ norλ_i may be cancelled unless a suitable injectivity hypothesis is established.','agrees','The final scalar equation is live and additional to the preceding incomplete normalization attempts.')
item(67,[67,112],'reading question',[124,126,128,130,132,138,142,144],['1018-1047','1106-1126','1201-1214','1274-1312','1415-1449','1754-1758','1939-1987','2021-2105'],'b=b1⊗b2; Si on a pu définir F11','The prior Hua register bundles distinct questions: integral local product, compatible morphisms, global coherence, invertible objects, and strict-endpoint pushout/pullback comparison. These sheets support the local coefficients and attempted cocycle; the comparison usesγχ=2 and conditionalF11.','The register itself is unavailable. Its T0-on-V(χ) criterion and full symmetric-monoidal claim are not stated here and must remain separate leads.','agrees','Entries67 and112 give the same scope audit. Neither an all-χ product nor its failure follows merely from the restricted comparison.')
item(68,[68,70],'reading question',[98,100,104,110,117,122],['64-76','124-154','279-305','574-607','863-877','984-1014'],'mod. loc. libre ... de rg2; deux torseurs ... sur les mod. inv.','These pages concern rank-two forms, quadratic/quasi-quadratic algebras, line-module torsors and extensions. They do not construct a conic, a form ofP1, a ternary-form similarity class, or a central degree-two Azumaya algebra.','This is a bounded negative finding from reading all48faces, not a claim about all cote84. The wordtorsor does not specifyPGL2.','agrees','The positive objects actually on the pages differ from the contextualB12 forecast. Entry70 is the same scope issue in the torsor wording.')
item(69,[69],'unpursued implication',[104,117,122],['279-305','863-877','984-1014'],'A1⊗A2','A1⊗A2 is a commutative rank-fourR-algebra. Over a nonzero base it cannot be a central degree-two AzumayaR-algebra, whose centre isR; its centre is the whole rank-four algebra.','This elementary exclusion is the earlier run’s comparison, not Grothendieck asserting an Azumaya connection.','agrees','The source’s commutative polynomial presentation fixes the object, regardless of its rank or norm.')
